// ─── GLOSSARY DATA ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const GLOSSARY_TERMS = [
  { term:"Anterior", def:"Toward the front of the jaw. Anterior teeth are the ones at the very front — usually the largest and most symmetrical." },
  { term:"Apex predator", def:"A predator at the top of the food chain with no natural predators of its own. Most large sharks qualify." },
  { term:"Auriculatus", def:"Refers to Otodus auriculatus, an ancient ancestor of Megalodon. Also describes the ear-like auricle projections found on the base of some teeth." },
  { term:"Auricles", def:"Small ear-shaped projections at the sides of the crown-root junction. Most pronounced in Otodus auriculatus teeth." },
  { term:"Bilobate root", def:"A root with two distinct lobes (like a wishbone or heart shape). Very common in large shark teeth including Megalodon, Great White, and Bull Shark." },
  { term:"Bourlette", def:"A chevron-shaped V-groove at the junction between crown and root. A key diagnostic feature of the Otodontidae family (Megalodon, Chubutensis, Auriculatus). Great White teeth do NOT have a bourlette." },
  { term:"Carcharhinidae", def:"The requiem shark family — the largest family of sharks, including Bull, Tiger, Reef, Hammerhead, and Lemon. Adult teeth have no cusplets." },
  { term:"Cockscomb notch", def:"A distinct step or indentation partway down the distal cutting edge of a Tiger Shark tooth. Completely diagnostic — no other common shark species has it." },
  { term:"Crown", def:"The visible pointed or blade-like part of a shark tooth above the root. This is what does the cutting." },
  { term:"Crown-root junction", def:"The boundary line between the crown and root of a tooth. This is where the bourlette appears in Otodontidae species." },
  { term:"Cusp", def:"A single pointed projection on a tooth. The main cusp is the large central point; lateral cusplets are smaller projections at the sides." },
  { term:"Cusplets", def:"Small triangular projections at the base of the main crown, one on each side. Their presence or absence is the single most powerful diagnostic feature in shark tooth ID." },
  { term:"Diagenesis", def:"The geological process by which organic material (like a tooth) is chemically replaced by minerals over time. This turns shark teeth black, gray, or brown as fossils." },
  { term:"Distal", def:"The side of a tooth facing toward the corner of the jaw (away from the midline). Opposite of mesial." },
  { term:"Elasmobranch", def:"The scientific group that includes all sharks, rays, and skates. They have cartilaginous skeletons and continually replace their teeth throughout their lives." },
  { term:"Enamel", def:"The hard outer coating of the crown. Shark tooth enamel (enameloid) is extremely hard — one reason shark teeth preserve so well as fossils." },
  { term:"Endothermic", def:"Able to regulate body temperature internally (warm-blooded). Great White and Mako sharks are endothermic, which is unusual for fish." },
  { term:"Euryhaline", def:"Able to tolerate a wide range of salt concentrations. Bull Sharks are famously euryhaline, able to survive in fresh water." },
  { term:"Extant", def:"Still alive today. The opposite of extinct. Great Whites, Tigers, and Bull Sharks are extant species." },
  { term:"Extinction", def:"When a species completely dies out with no living members. Megalodon, Chubutensis, and Auriculatus are all extinct." },
  { term:"Fossil", def:"The preserved remains of an ancient organism. Shark teeth are the most common vertebrate fossil because sharks produce thousands of teeth in a lifetime." },
  { term:"Galeocerdo cuvier", def:"The scientific name for the Tiger Shark — the only living member of its genus. Famous for its cockscomb notch and willingness to eat almost anything." },
  { term:"Genus", def:"A classification group above species. For example, Carcharhinus is the genus for Bull, Reef, and Dusky sharks. Always capitalized and italicized in scientific writing." },
  { term:"Hemigaleidae", def:"The weasel shark family. Hemipristis (Snaggletooth) belongs to this family, distinguished by serrations on only one cutting edge." },
  { term:"Hexanchidae", def:"The cow shark family — one of the most primitive shark families still alive. Recognizable by their multi-cusped, comb-like teeth." },
  { term:"IUCN", def:"International Union for Conservation of Nature. They publish the Red List of threatened species. Statuses like Vulnerable, Endangered, and Critically Endangered come from IUCN assessments." },
  { term:"Labial face", def:"The outer (lip-facing) face of a tooth. In most shark teeth, the labial face is flatter than the lingual face." },
  { term:"Lamnidae", def:"The mackerel shark family, which includes the Great White, Mako, Salmon Shark, and Porbeagle. Characterized by smooth or coarsely-serrated teeth without cusplets." },
  { term:"Lateral", def:"Toward the sides. Lateral teeth are found toward the corners of the jaw, away from the front teeth." },
  { term:"Lingual face", def:"The inner (tongue-facing) face of a tooth. Usually more convex (curved outward) than the labial face." },
  { term:"Ma", def:"Abbreviation for million years ago (from the Latin mega-annum). Used when describing the geological age of fossils or species." },
  { term:"Mesial", def:"The side of a tooth facing toward the midline of the jaw. Opposite of distal." },
  { term:"Miocene", def:"A geological epoch from about 5-23 million years ago. Many commonly found shark tooth fossils, including Megalodon, date to this period." },
  { term:"Mineralization", def:"The process by which organic material is replaced by minerals during fossilization. This is why fossil shark teeth are often black, dark gray, or brown." },
  { term:"Morphology", def:"The shape and structure of a tooth. Tooth morphology is the primary way to identify what species a tooth came from." },
  { term:"Nutritive groove", def:"A groove on the root that allows blood vessels to nourish the developing tooth. Especially prominent in Hammerhead shark teeth." },
  { term:"Oblique", def:"Strongly tilted or leaning to one side. Tiger Shark and Hemipristis teeth are highly oblique compared to the more upright Megalodon or Great White." },
  { term:"Odontaspididae", def:"The sand tiger shark family. Known for narrow, elongated teeth with prominent lateral cusplets and completely smooth cutting edges." },
  { term:"Otodontidae", def:"The extinct megatooth shark family that includes Megalodon, Chubutensis, and Auriculatus. Distinguished by large size, fine serrations, and a bourlette at the crown-root junction." },
  { term:"Paleontology", def:"The scientific study of ancient life through fossils. Shark tooth collectors contribute to paleontology every time they find and document a tooth." },
  { term:"Phosphate mineralization", def:"The specific type of mineralization most common in shark teeth, where calcium phosphate replaces the original organic material, turning teeth dark." },
  { term:"Pliocene", def:"A geological epoch from about 2.6-5.3 million years ago. Megalodon went extinct near the end of this period." },
  { term:"Root", def:"The lower portion of a shark tooth that anchors it to the jaw. The root shape (bilobate, flat, narrow, curved) is an important identification feature." },
  { term:"Serrations", def:"The small tooth-like bumps along the cutting edges of a shark tooth crown. Can be fine (small and regular) or coarse (large and irregular). Their presence, absence, and pattern are key ID features." },
  { term:"Species", def:"The most specific classification level for organisms. Members of the same species can reproduce with each other. Written in lowercase italic after the genus name." },
  { term:"Taxonomy", def:"The science of classifying and naming living organisms. Shark taxonomy determines how different species are grouped into families, genera, and species." },
  { term:"Tooth position", def:"Where in the jaw a tooth comes from — anterior (front), lateral (side), or posterior (back). Position affects the tooth shape and can help narrow down identification." },
];


